---
/**
 * /weather/limits — what this map can and cannot tell you.
 *
 * The order matters and was got wrong once. This page used to open by denying
 * that the map is a probability, which meant the first thing a forager read
 * was a disowning. It opens now with the measured claim, because that is the
 * thing that is true and useful, and the qualifier follows it in one section
 * rather than standing over the whole page.
 *
 * The spread between the best- and worst-evidenced species is read from the
 * validation record rather than written, so it cannot go stale here.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SpeciesRoster from '../../components/weather/SpeciesRoster.astro';
import {
  currentRun,
  evidentialStatus,
  headlineClaim,
  lookAlikeCoverage,
  profiles,
  speciesWithBareLookAlikes,
  validationCaveat,
  validationGenerated,
} from '../../lib/weather';

const run = currentRun;
const claim = headlineClaim(run ?? undefined);
const coverage = lookAlikeCoverage();

// The spread, stated from the record rather than from memory.
const statuses = Object.values(profiles).map((p) => ({ p, s: evidentialStatus(p.id) }));
const measured = statuses.filter((x) => x.s.kind === 'measured' && x.s.entry);
const unmeasured = statuses.filter((x) => x.s.kind !== 'measured' && x.s.entry);

const best = measured.length
  ? measured.reduce((a, b) => ((b.s.entry!.n_obs ?? 0) > (a.s.entry!.n_obs ?? 0) ? b : a))
  : null;
const worst = unmeasured.length
  ? unmeasured.reduce((a, b) =>
      (b.s.entry!.max_observed_score ?? 1) < (a.s.entry!.max_observed_score ?? 1) ? b : a
    )
  : null;
const excluded = statuses.filter((x) => x.s.kind === 'excluded');

const parts: string[] = [];
if (best) {
  parts.push(
    `${best.p.common_name ?? best.p.id} has been checked against ${best.s.entry!.n_obs} records ` +
      `with ${best.s.entry!.n_control} effort-matched controls`
  );
}
if (excluded.length) {
  parts.push(
    `${excluded[0].p.common_name ?? excluded[0].p.id} cannot be back-tested at all, because ` +
      'almost every record of it carries a deliberately displaced location'
  );
}
if (worst) {
  parts.push(
    `no real record of ${worst.p.common_name ?? worst.p.id} has ever sat on ground scoring above ` +
      `${(worst.s.entry!.max_observed_score ?? 0).toFixed(3)}`
  );
}
const spread = parts.length
  ? parts.join('; ') + '.'
  : 'Nothing has been back-tested yet, so no species here is better evidenced than any other.';

const bare = speciesWithBareLookAlikes();
const totalBare = bare.reduce((n, b) => n + b.bare.length, 0);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What the fruiting map can and can't tell you | MycoSci"
  description="The measured claim behind the colours, the one thing this map cannot do, and which species have actually been back-tested."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10">
    <div class="border-b border-lab-800 pb-8 mb-10">
      <a href="/weather" class="label text-bio-500 hover:text-bio-400">// Fruiting weather</a>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold text-lab-100 mt-2 tracking-tight">
        What it can and can't tell you
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lab-400 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The measured claim first, then the honest list — in the order the mistakes actually get
        made.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-sm max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:text-lab-100 prose-p:text-lab-400 prose-li:text-lab-400 prose-strong:text-lab-200 prose-a:text-bio-400 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:text-bio-300">
      <h2>What it can tell you</h2>
      {
        claim ? (
          <>
            <p>
              This is a foraging map, and it makes one measured claim. Ground scoring{' '}
              <strong>{Number(claim.top.min).toFixed(2)} or better</strong> has turned up{' '}
              {claim.speciesName} about <strong>{claim.top.enrichment.toFixed(1)} times as often</strong>{' '}
              as ordinary ground people search — measured across {claim.nObs} real occurrence
              records against {claim.nControl} effort-matched controls.
            </p>
            <ul>
              {claim.bands.map((b) => (
                <li>
                  <strong>{b.enrichment.toFixed(1)}×</strong> — {b.meaning}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <p>
              That is a real, checkable statement about where to spend a morning, and it is the
              product. Everything below is the fine print on it.
            </p>
          </>
        ) : (
          <p>
            No species here has been back-tested against real occurrence records yet, so there is
            no measured ratio to quote. Every map is the model's own output — a hypothesis about
            where to look, with nothing yet standing behind its ranking.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <h2>It does not identify mushrooms</h2>
      <p>
        This is the one hard limit, and it is the one where being wrong hurts somebody. A map is
        about ground. What is in your hand is a different question and this site does not answer
        it. Some species mapped here have look-alikes that will put you in hospital, and at least
        one common Oregon look-alike causes flushing, nausea and palpitations — but only if you
        drink alcohol within a day or two of eating it. Use a key, take a spore print, and check
        with somebody who already knows the species.
      </p>
      {
        coverage.researched.length > 0 && (
          <p>
            What this section does carry is the content that a disclaimer cannot replace.{' '}
            {coverage.researched.length} of the {coverage.total} species profiles carry a
            look-alike review written from {coverage.sourcesMin}–{coverage.sourcesMax} independent
            sources each — what it is confused with, how the sources say to tell them apart, and,
            where reputable sources contradict each other, the contradiction left standing rather
            than resolved:{' '}
            {coverage.researched.map((p, i) => (
              <>{i > 0 ? ', ' : ''}<a href={`/weather/species/${p.id}#look-alikes`}>{p.common_name ?? p.id}</a></>
            ))}
          </p>
        )
      }
      {
        bare.length > 0 && (
          <p>
            <strong>And some of the look-alike data is still incomplete.</strong> {totalBare}{' '}
            entries across {bare.length} species are recorded as bare identifiers — a name, with
            no description, no symptoms and no way to tell the two apart:{' '}
            {bare.map((b) => b.name).join(', ')}. Those are shown marked as incomplete rather than
            dressed up as guidance. Do not use this section as a look-alike check for them.
          </p>
        )
      }

      <h2>It is a ratio, not a percentage</h2>
      {validationCaveat && <p>{validationCaveat}</p>}
      <p>
        Put plainly: to say “there is a 40% chance of finding chanterelles here today” you need to
        know how often people looked and found nothing. Nobody records that. Every occurrence
        database on earth is a record of successes. So the strongest true statement available is a{' '}
        <em>ratio</em> — this ground holds real records several times more often than ground people
        searched just as hard — and that is the statement this section makes. More sample size does
        not fix it. It is not a precision problem; the number simply is not in the data.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which is worth being clear about in both directions. It means the ratio
        {claim ? ` — ${claim.top.enrichment.toFixed(1)}× — ` : ' '}is not a promise of mushrooms.
        It also means the comparison is against ground people <em>actually search</em>, not
        against random wilderness — so it is a harder test than it sounds, and passing it is worth
        something.
      </p>

      <h2>Species have not been checked equally</h2>
      <p>
        Every species here gets the same model and the same map. The difference is the back-test,
        and it is not a matter of degree. {spread} They all produce a map that looks equally
        confident. They have not all been checked.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8">
      <SpeciesRoster showRunMark={false} />
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-lab-500 leading-relaxed">
      Standing band analysis dated {validationGenerated || 'unknown'}. None of it is used to label
      any map in this section.
    </p>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-sm max-w-none mt-10 prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:text-lab-100 prose-p:text-lab-400 prose-li:text-lab-400 prose-strong:text-lab-200 prose-a:text-bio-400 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:text-bio-300">
      <h2>One species can't be back-tested, because its records are scrambled</h2>
      <p>
        iNaturalist automatically displaces the location of species that get poached, and matsutake
        is one of them. That is a statement about the records, not about the map: a back-test run
        on scrambled coordinates measures the scrambling. The map is still the model's real output
        for matsutake; there is simply no way yet to say how well it ranks, and publishing a number
        produced from ruined data would be worse than saying so.
      </p>

      <h2>A weather model has no mycelium in it</h2>
      <p>
        The score reads weather, elevation and land cover. It does not know whether there is
        mycelium under a given hillside, whether the stand was logged last spring, whether the patch
        was picked out yesterday morning, or whether the ground is private, closed or dangerous.
        Bright ground is ground where the <em>weather</em> is right.
      </p>

      <h2>It is one day behind</h2>
      <p>
        The weather archive this reads settles about a day after the fact. A morning run in Pacific
        time lands somewhere between fourteen and thirty-eight hours behind real time, and every
        page here leads with the date the weather actually knows rather than the date the map is
        labelled for. If a storm arrived this morning, this map has not heard about it.
        {run?.lag_days_behind_target
          ? ` The current run is ${run.lag_days_behind_target} day behind its own label.`
          : ''}
      </p>

      <h2>The report does not describe the weather</h2>
      <p>
        The daily report says what moved in the score distributions between runs. It does not say
        how much rain fell or where, because the manifest it is built from does not record any
        weather values — only the provenance of the fetch and the distributions that came out the
        far end. A rainfall sentence would have to be imagined, so none is written.
      </p>

      <h2>It only publishes the current day</h2>
      <p>
        One run at a time. There is no image archive and no forecast: past runs exist here as
        numbers — score distributions and provenance, kept in full — but their maps are not
        republished, and no map is ever drawn for a day the pipeline did not produce. Historical and
        forecast projections are a later phase.
      </p>

      <h2>Oregon only</h2>
      <p>
        The land-cover layer stops at the United States border and the weather grid is drawn for
        this state. Outside it, the habitat gate is never assessed and the map means nothing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
